-- ChatGPT auto viral article: Waffle House Waco
WITH item AS (SELECT '2026-08-09-waffle-house-waco'::text slug,'Viral'::text category,'Waffle House Picks Waco for Its Next Texas Expansion While San Antonio Keeps Waiting'::text title,'A state filing points to a new Waco Waffle House planned for 2027, giving Central Texas another location while San Antonio remains a conspicuous gap in the chain’s map.'::text dek,'MySA'::text source_name,'https://www.mysanantonio.com/food/article/waffle-house-waco-22363338.php'::text source_url,'2026-08-09T15:36:00Z'::timestamptz published_at,26::int score,FALSE::boolean is_breaking,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/waffle-house-waco.svg'::text image_url,'Editorial illustration of a roadside diner in Waco beside a waffle-grid sunrise and Texas highway.'::text image_alt_text,$body$Waffle House is planning a new location in Waco, giving Central Texas breakfast fans another yellow-sign destination while San Antonio continues to wait for one of its own. MySA reported that a state construction filing lists a roughly 1,800-square-foot restaurant at 2301 S. New Road in Waco. The filing estimates a $300,000 project with construction scheduled to begin March 1, 2027 and finish August 1, 2027, although construction filings are preliminary and dates can change.

The Waco location is notable partly because of geography. The closest current Waffle House to Waco is in Hillsboro, about 34 miles north, according to the report. Waco sits on Interstate 35 between Austin and the Dallas-Fort Worth region, giving the chain access to local residents, Baylor University traffic and a constant stream of highway travelers. A 24-hour diner brand built around road-trip familiarity is a natural fit for a corridor where travelers already recognize the sign before they recognize the exit.

The more entertaining part of the story is San Antonio. The city is one of the largest in the country, yet Waffle House has never established the kind of footprint there that it has in Houston, Dallas-Fort Worth and other Texas markets. MySA noted that the nearest location to San Antonio is around Kyle, roughly 60 miles away. Every new filing elsewhere in the state therefore tends to trigger the same local reaction: if Waco is getting one, why not San Antonio?

That reaction has become a recurring piece of Texas food culture because Waffle House inspires an attachment that is disproportionate to a simple menu of eggs, hash browns and waffles. The chain’s appeal is consistency. A traveler walking into a Waffle House at 2 a.m. generally knows what the room will look like, what the grill will sound like and how the hash browns can be ordered. That familiarity turns expansion announcements into local status symbols for communities that do not yet have a restaurant.

Texas already has more than 130 Waffle House locations, according to the MySA report, so the chain is hardly absent from the state. Its distribution is simply uneven. The company has also been associated with 2026 project filings in Fort Worth, Anna and Splendora. Those markets offer different combinations of population growth, highway access and development costs. San Antonio’s absence may therefore be less about demand than about site selection, franchise strategy, real estate and the chain’s preferred operating geography.

Waco itself has changed rapidly enough to make the project unsurprising. The city has grown as a tourism destination and continues to attract retail and restaurant investment along the I-35 corridor. A relatively small diner can capture both local repeat customers and interstate traffic. The modest size listed in the filing is also typical of a format that does not need a huge dining room to operate around the clock.

Construction filings should still be treated as plans rather than guarantees. Developers can change schedules, budgets and even locations after documents are filed with the state. A 2027 completion date means there is plenty of time for revisions. But filings are usually the first concrete public sign of a chain’s intent, which is why restaurant watchers pay attention to them long before a coming-soon sign appears on a lot.

If the project moves forward, the opening will give Waco another recognizable national brand without changing the city’s strong local breakfast scene. Chain restaurants and local institutions often serve different customers and occasions. A Waffle House near a major traffic corridor may compete more directly for late-night travelers and quick breakfast customers than for people seeking a destination brunch.

For San Antonio fans, the Waco announcement will mostly prolong a running joke. Rumors about a first local Waffle House have circulated before, and online speculation has repeatedly outrun confirmed plans. Until a real filing or company announcement names a San Antonio site, the closest plate of scattered, smothered or covered hash browns remains a drive up Interstate 35. Waco, meanwhile, appears to be moving one step closer.

The announcement also lands in a part of Texas where restaurant expansion tracks population and highway growth closely. Waco has become more than a midpoint rest stop, but its interstate location still gives national chains an unusually broad customer base. A diner that serves breakfast around the clock can pull from students, hospital workers, truck drivers, tourists and late-night travelers without depending on one narrow daypart. That mix helps explain why a small footprint can be economically useful in a high-traffic corridor and why restaurant filings along I-35 draw attention well before construction begins.

This article is an independent Keep TX Red rewrite based on MySA reporting published July 29, 2026. The construction cost and timetable come from a state filing and should be considered preliminary until Waffle House or the developer confirms construction and an opening date.$body$::text body), prepared AS (SELECT *,jsonb_build_object('updated','2026-08-09','intro',jsonb_build_array(split_part(body,E'\n\n',1)),'sections',jsonb_build_array(jsonb_build_object('heading','The story','paragraphs',jsonb_build_array(body))),'faq',jsonb_build_array(),'sources',jsonb_build_array(jsonb_build_object('label',source_name||' — original report','url',source_url)),'keyTakeaways',jsonb_build_array(dek)) body_json FROM item) INSERT INTO public.daily_articles(slug,internal_url,is_ingested,category,title,dek,source_name,source_url,published_at,score,is_breaking,kind,body,body_json,featured_image_url,image_url,image_alt_text) SELECT slug,'/news/'||slug,FALSE,category,title,dek,source_name,source_url,published_at,score,is_breaking,'news',body,body_json,image_url,image_url,image_alt_text FROM prepared ON CONFLICT(slug) DO UPDATE SET category=EXCLUDED.category,title=EXCLUDED.title,dek=EXCLUDED.dek,source_name=EXCLUDED.source_name,source_url=EXCLUDED.source_url,published_at=EXCLUDED.published_at,score=EXCLUDED.score,is_breaking=EXCLUDED.is_breaking,body=EXCLUDED.body,body_json=EXCLUDED.body_json,featured_image_url=EXCLUDED.featured_image_url,image_url=EXCLUDED.image_url,image_alt_text=EXCLUDED.image_alt_text;